{"authored" [{"question" "q-350978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=14{t}^{6}+12{t}^{3},$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t)$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=310\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "e39c26b45208628dfd3fc441fcf876c2eee59912cc354b70df3aa44388f5ae56" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-350978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (14{t}^{6}+12{t}^{3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{7}+3{t}^{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(2)=310.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}310 & =2({2}^{7})+3({2}^{4})+C \\\\ 310 & =304+C \\\\ C & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{7}+3{t}^{4}+6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "e39c26b45208628dfd3fc441fcf876c2eee59912cc354b70df3aa44388f5ae56" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-350978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "97bf1afa6ceed68565479fe95947852ea820cfb8ac2cec4ad8694941a33898d7" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-350978" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/state.json" "file_sha256" "e39c26b45208628dfd3fc441fcf876c2eee59912cc354b70df3aa44388f5ae56" "basis" 1156 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010777/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-7112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2t-9{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=-2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-7112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t-9{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =(1)^{2}-3(1)^{3}+C \\\\ -2 & =1-3+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(2)^{2}-3(2)^{3} \\\\  & =4-24 \\\\  & =-20\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $20\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-7112" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8b2f8408a328e5b806c1e628bc71ca6c654340d1174eadb0a141f5e98abbefdb"} {"question" "q-7112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-7112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "16\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "20\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "24\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "4\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-7112" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-19486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. Given that it is at rest when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, find the velocity (in $\\text{m/s}$)  of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-19486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-19486" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8b2f8408a328e5b806c1e628bc71ca6c654340d1174eadb0a141f5e98abbefdb"} {"question" "q-19486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-19486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] ["math" "v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-1)"] ["math" "v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-1)"] ["math" "v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-19486" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-19489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t),$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{4}{5}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-19489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4(-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{4}{5}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot 0)+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot 1+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t).$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =-0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.json" "file_sha256" "ba3e3eeedee886545a79a71018c23daf2987e2a1f746029771da26d1bea2b3f6"} {"question" "q-19489" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8b2f8408a328e5b806c1e628bc71ca6c654340d1174eadb0a141f5e98abbefdb"} {"question" "q-19489" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14063982/state.js